### What does `this` refer to inside an anonymous class?

An anonymous class is a real class with its own instance, so inside its body `this` naturally denotes that instance. If the anonymous class implements `Runnable`, then inside `run()` the expression `this` is the `Runnable` object.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TransferService {
    private String channel = "NEFT";

    public void demonstrateThis() {
        Runnable task = new Runnable() {
            @Override
            public void run() {
                // 'this' is the anonymous Runnable instance
                System.out.println("this class: " + this.getClass().getSimpleName());
                System.out.println("this identity: " + System.identityHashCode(this));
            }
        };
        task.run();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new TransferService().demonstrateThis();
    }
}
```

```text
this class: 
this identity: 1234567890
```

The class name is empty because the anonymous class has no simple name. The identity hash confirms `this` is the anonymous object, not the `TransferService`.

### How do you reference the enclosing instance?

Java provides the qualified `this` syntax: `OuterClassName.this`. If the enclosing class is `TransferService`, then inside the anonymous class, `TransferService.this` gives you the enclosing `TransferService` object.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TransferService {
    private String channel = "NEFT";

    public void registerInRegistry() {
        Runnable task = new Runnable() {
            @Override
            public void run() {
                // Access enclosing instance via qualified this
                TransferService outer = TransferService.this;
                System.out.println("Outer channel: " + outer.channel);
                System.out.println("Outer identity: " + System.identityHashCode(outer));
                System.out.println("Anon identity:  " + System.identityHashCode(this));
            }
        };
        task.run();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new TransferService().registerInRegistry();
    }
}
```

```text
Outer channel: NEFT
Outer identity: 111111111
Anon identity:  222222222
```

### How does field and method shadowing work?

When both the enclosing class and the anonymous class define a member with the same name, `this.name` resolves to the anonymous member and `Outer.this.name` resolves to the outer member.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class NotificationService {
    private String channel = "EMAIL";

    public void sendNotification() {
        Runnable task = new Runnable() {
            private String channel = "SMS"; // shadows outer field

            @Override
            public void run() {
                System.out.println("Anonymous channel: " + this.channel);
                System.out.println("Outer channel:     " + NotificationService.this.channel);
            }
        };
        task.run();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new NotificationService().sendNotification();
    }
}
```

```text
Anonymous channel: SMS
Outer channel:     EMAIL
```

Without the qualified form, you could not reach the shadowed outer field.

### When can you access outer members without qualification?

If the outer class has a unique field or method that the anonymous class does not redefine, you can access it directly by name. Qualification is only required when there is a naming clash or when you want to pass the outer object itself as a reference.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class AuditService {
    private String auditLog = "AUDIT_LOG_2024";

    public void createTask() {
        Runnable task = new Runnable() {
            @Override
            public void run() {
                // No shadowing: auditLog resolves to the outer field directly
                System.out.println("Using log: " + auditLog);
            }
        };
        task.run();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new AuditService().createTask();
    }
}
```

```text
Using log: AUDIT_LOG_2024
```

### How is `this` different in an anonymous class vs a lambda?

A lambda does not create its own `this` scope. Inside a lambda, `this` refers to the enclosing instance directly, exactly as it would in the surrounding method. This is the opposite of an anonymous class, where `this` is the anonymous object.

| Feature | Anonymous Class | Lambda |
|---|---|---|
| `this` refers to | The anonymous object | The enclosing instance |
| Access outer `this` | `Outer.this` | `this` (already the outer) |
| Own identity | Has its own object identity | No separate object identity |

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class ThisComparison {
    private String name = "TransferService";

    public void compare() {
        // Anonymous class: this = anonymous object
        Runnable anon = new Runnable() {
            @Override
            public void run() {
                // this.name would fail: Runnable has no 'name'
                System.out.println("Anon this:  " + this.getClass().getName());
            }
        };

        // Lambda: this = enclosing ThisComparison instance
        Runnable lambda = () -> {
            System.out.println("Lambda this: " + this.getClass().getName());
            System.out.println("Lambda name: " + this.name);
        };

        anon.run();
        lambda.run();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new ThisComparison().compare();
    }
}
```

```text
Anon this:  com.bank.service.ThisComparison$1
Lambda this: com.bank.service.ThisComparison
Lambda name: TransferService
```

### What happens in a static context?

If the anonymous class is created inside a static method, there is no enclosing instance. Using `Outer.this` is a compile error because only instance methods and instance initializers have an enclosing instance.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class StaticContextDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Runnable task = new Runnable() {
            @Override
            public void run() {
                // StaticContextDemo.this is NOT available here
                // because main() is static
                System.out.println("No enclosing instance in static context");
            }
        };
        task.run();
    }
}
```

### What are the memory implications of capturing Outer.this?

Capturing `Outer.this` keeps the outer object alive as long as the anonymous instance lives. In banking systems where event listeners may be registered for the application lifetime, this can cause subtle memory leaks.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class ListenerLeakDemo {
    private static final List<Runnable> registry = new ArrayList<>();
    private BigDecimal[] largeCache = new BigDecimal[100_000];

    public void registerListener() {
        // The anonymous class holds a reference to this ListenerLeakDemo
        // instance, keeping largeCache alive even if nothing else references it
        registry.add(new Runnable() {
            @Override
            public void run() {
                System.out.println("Listener for: " + ListenerLeakDemo.this);
            }
        });
    }
}
```

Pankaj, the architect at National Bank of India, recommends using static nested classes or lambdas for long-lived listeners to avoid holding the enclosing instance unnecessarily.

### What should a senior engineer know?

The `this` reference behaviour is a key factor when choosing between an anonymous class and a lambda. If your callback needs its own identity (for example, to register and later unregister itself from a listener registry using `==` comparison), an anonymous class gives you that distinct `this`. If you just need to capture the enclosing service's state, a lambda's `this` already points to the enclosing instance, which is simpler and avoids the qualified form.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

public class RegistryPattern {
    private final Set<Runnable> listeners = new HashSet<>();

    public void addAndRemove() {
        Runnable listener = new Runnable() {
            @Override
            public void run() {
                System.out.println("Processing Priya's account event");
            }
        };

        listeners.add(listener);
        System.out.println("Registered: " + listeners.size());

        // Can remove because we hold the same 'this' reference
        listeners.remove(listener);
        System.out.println("After removal: " + listeners.size());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new RegistryPattern().addAndRemove();
    }
}
```

```text
Registered: 1
After removal: 0
```
